  "sceneDirections": [
    {
      "sourceLine": 3,
      "title": "Returns and the decision",
      "direction": "Kiva reads eleven returns: eight negative, Westerley, Tarnfoot, Holloway. No fever recorded is not proof of no fever. Noa records two hairs, 12:44 Wednesday and 12:52 Thursday morning, without claiming cause. His fourth column is ash. Holloway changes Kiva’s understanding of a second’s silence: it must end in testimony. The sealed page establishes independent words, not an explanation of the sky. Her decision is quiet and costly, with no prophetic grandeur."
    },
    {
      "sourceLine": 89,
      "title": "The four at Quiller’s",
      "direction": "At eight Thursday, Kiva tells Pru, Jude, Anwen and Perrin before the Board. Keep her description of the dream grounded and unadorned. She withholds part of the ferry deliberately; do not imply she has disclosed white lights. Pru’s blush carries trust and curiosity, never childishness. Perrin distinguishes a thing she does from a thing she is; pain limits breath but never narrator identity. Jude respects ownership; Anwen promises presence. The book records solidarity, not the private dream."
    },
    {
      "sourceLine": 187,
      "title": "Mara teaches and sends the wires",
      "direction": "Reset focalization to Mara at ten Thursday. Three training cells are separate from the thirty-six bank cells: own count, Peverell’s rug, town. Twelve fitters are the first of three watches. Town or mine is a report, not an order to retune. Mara will watch rather than become one engine. Kiva asks for the page; Mara’s unspoken fears must remain interior, never voiced as dialogue. Mara supports a choice she does not own. The Sunday wire anticipates transit and the Board’s schedule. Mine at the training cell is private ownership of her action, not a bank already changed."
    },
    {
      "sourceLine": 295,
      "title": "Aske’s window",
      "direction": "Friday seven; five warm mornings. Aske’s pencil inferred recurrence, not dream content. Care is an offered action without a price. Quiet and exact, without a doctor caricature."
    },
    {
      "sourceLine": 311,
      "title": "Orla at the mast",
      "direction": "Friday half past seven. Orla and Kiva acknowledge their separate Annick readings. Orla read the Ferren and Saltmere entries but did not know Kiva dreams. Neither certificate nor sponsorship owns this disclosure. Her warning against doctrine is personal and practical; return to the coming one-door lane, without a sermon."
    }
  ],
